</span></span></span>
<span id="A3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Doc 1: (Title: List of Starship launches - Wikipedia) … mission slated for launch to the moon in 2027 as part of SpaceX’s first Starship HLS mission for the Artemis program. An unknown number of refueling …</span></span>
<span id="A3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Doc 2: (Title: SpaceX Starship - Wikipedia) The first launch of a full Starship vehicle occurred on April 20, 2023, and ended with the explosion of the rocket four minutes after liftoff. The program has …</span></span>
<span id="A3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Doc 3: (Title: Artemis I - NASA) Launch date: Nov. 16, 2022 ; Mission duration: 25 days, 10 hours, 53 minutes ; Total distance traveled: 1.4 million miles ; Re-entry speed: 24,581 mph (Mach 32).</span></span>
<span id="A3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SSx2.SSSx1.p2.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Doc 4: (Title: Starship overview - SpaceX) Starship is the world’s most powerful launch vehicle ever developed, capable of carrying up to 150 metric tonnes fully reusable and 250 metric tonnes expendable …</span></span>
